Article 25

1. The Regional Government is obliged to protect the fulfillment of the rights of child workers in the informal sector.
2. Child Workers in the informal sector as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a domestic workers;

b shoe shiners;

c street vendors;

d scavengers;

e parking attendants;

f children working in agriculture, animal husbandry and plantations;

g children working in mining; and h other informal sector work that employs children.  (3) Every person who employs children in the informal sector must pay attention to the requirements: a minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the category of Worst Forms of Child Labor minimum 18 (eighteen) years;

b obtain written consent from the parents/guardian of the child worker;  c have a written employment agreement between the employer and the parents/guardian of the child worker and obtain validation from the authorized agency;

d not employed at night;

e working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;

f not employed in a place or environment that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;

g provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;

h employ for light types of work; and  i provide one day off per week.

Article 30

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 27 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/ataud. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of Women and Children, is implemented based on the principles of: a. respect and protection of Human Rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of the Child;
e. right to life, survival, and development; f. empowerment; andg. legal certainty.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of women and children;
b. maintain the integrity of the household to realize a harmonious family;
c. prevent violence against women and children;
d. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; e. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. provide protection to reporters and witnesses; and g. empower women victims of violence.

Article 4

Every Woman has the right: a to life and the right to maintain life and livelihood;

b to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage;

c to develop themselves through the fulfillment of their basic needs; d to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, to improve the quality of life and for the welfare of humanity; e to advance themselves in fighting for their rights collectively to build society, nation and state; f to recognition, guarantee, protection and fair legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;

g to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;

h to obtain equal opportunities in government; i to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion; j to protection of self, family, honor, dignity and property under their control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something that is a basic right;

k to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from other countries;

l to live prosperously physically and spiritually, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to obtain health services;

m to social security that enables their full development as dignified human beings;

n to equality of position in law and government;

o to work and a livelihood worthy of humanity; p in the defense of the state;

q to communicate and obtain information to develop their personal and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;

r to embrace their respective religions and to worship according to their religion and beliefs; and s to obtain education.

Article 11

1. To implement Women's Empowerment as referred to in Article 6, the Regional Government shall organize gender mainstreaming including: a establishing the implementation of gender mainstreaming;

b coordinating, facilitating, and mediating gender mainstreaming;

c facilitating institutional strengthening and development of gender mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;

d implementing gender mainstreaming related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

e improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

f integrating efforts to improve the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

g coordinating the implementation of improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

h organizing the protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

i facilitating the integration of women's protection especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

j coordinating the protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities;

k facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business world for the implementation of gender mainstreaming;

l developing a gender-based information system; and m providing and organizing integrated or non-integrated services for women victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities.

2. Further provisions regarding the forms of women's empowerment and gender mainstreaming mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 34

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women and children shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government and coordinated by the technical SKPD.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by:a. providing guidance towards a harmonious household integrity;
b. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence; c. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
d. establishing a violence prevention system;
e. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; andf. providing critical education regarding the rights of women and children for the community.

Article 31

Neglect as referred to in Article 27 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to meet the needs of children properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or take care of children as they should, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide life, care, or maintenance to that person; and/ataud. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting decent work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

CHAPTER VI

PROHIBITIONS

Article 47

1. Every business operation whose activities may interfere with the growth and development of children is prohibited from receiving child visitors.
2. Business operations as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a discotheques;

b nightclubs;

c bars;

d adult karaoke;

e pubs;

f massage parlors;

g steam bath/sauna parlors; and h other similar business fields.

