f. maintaining the confidentiality of victims; andg. providing fulfillment of legal assistance for victims.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures and mechanisms for implementing the PPT shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 37

Forms of handling of women and children victims of violence include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. medical rehabilitation services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services; andf. social reintegration services.

Article 46

Women and children victims of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 45, also receive special rights, as follows:
a. the right to survival;
b. the right to grow and develop;
c. the right to receive equal protection; and d. the right to participate in community life.

Article 4

Every Woman has the right: a to life and the right to maintain life and livelihood;

b to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage;

c to develop themselves through the fulfillment of their basic needs; d to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, to improve the quality of life and for the welfare of humanity; e to advance themselves in fighting for their rights collectively to build society, nation and state; f to recognition, guarantee, protection and fair legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;

g to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;

h to obtain equal opportunities in government; i to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion; j to protection of self, family, honor, dignity and property under their control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something that is a basic right;

k to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from other countries;

l to live prosperously physically and spiritually, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to obtain health services;

m to social security that enables their full development as dignified human beings;

n to equality of position in law and government;

o to work and a livelihood worthy of humanity; p in the defense of the state;

q to communicate and obtain information to develop their personal and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;

r to embrace their respective religions and to worship according to their religion and beliefs; and s to obtain education.

Article 24

1. The implementation of Protection and empowerment of children in the field of social welfare as referred to in Article 13 letter d, the Regional Government is obliged to organize social welfare for:a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of abduction and child victims of trafficking in persons;
c. child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse (NAPZA);
d. children infected with HIV/AIDS;
e. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;
f. street children;
g. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters;
h. children with disabilities;
i. children of migrant worker families;
j. children living in or around prostitution locations; and  k. child victims of other maltreatment.
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services: a health;

b education;

c legal aid;

d assistance;

e social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f social rehabilitation;

g empowerment;

h social assistance; and/or i reintegration.

