h. children with disabilities;
i. children of migrant worker families;
j. children living in or around prostitution locations; and  k. child victims of other maltreatment.
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services: a health;

b education;

c legal aid;

d assistance;

e social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f social rehabilitation;

g empowerment;

h social assistance; and/or i reintegration.

Article 39

Assistance services as referred to in Article 37 letter b include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
c. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. protecting the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by the mass media; e. providing a sense of security to victims; andf. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 26

Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in Article 25 is in the form of: a counseling to stakeholders about children's rights;

b assistance with psychological, medical and legal services and socio-economic reintegration;

c empowering families through provision of training, business capital stimulus and assistance;

d scholarships to continue formal education to higher levels; and e non-formal education and skills training for those who do not pursue formal education.

