e parking attendants;

f children working in agriculture, animal husbandry and plantations;

g children working in mining; and h other informal sector work that employs children.  (3) Every person who employs children in the informal sector must pay attention to the requirements: a minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the category of Worst Forms of Child Labor minimum 18 (eighteen) years;

b obtain written consent from the parents/guardian of the child worker;  c have a written employment agreement between the employer and the parents/guardian of the child worker and obtain validation from the authorized agency;

d not employed at night;

e working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;

f not employed in a place or environment that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;

g provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;

h employ for light types of work; and  i provide one day off per week.

Article 11

1. To implement Women's Empowerment as referred to in Article 6, the Regional Government shall organize gender mainstreaming including: a establishing the implementation of gender mainstreaming;

b coordinating, facilitating, and mediating gender mainstreaming;

c facilitating institutional strengthening and development of gender mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;

d implementing gender mainstreaming related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

e improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

f integrating efforts to improve the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

g coordinating the implementation of improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

h organizing the protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

i facilitating the integration of women's protection especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

j coordinating the protection of women especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities;

k facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business world for the implementation of gender mainstreaming;

l developing a gender-based information system; and m providing and organizing integrated or non-integrated services for women victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities.

2. Further provisions regarding the forms of women's empowerment and gender mainstreaming mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of women and children;
b. maintain the integrity of the household to realize a harmonious family;
c. prevent violence against women and children;
d. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; e. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. provide protection to reporters and witnesses; and g. empower women victims of violence.

CHAPTER IV

INTEGRATED SERVICE CENTER

Article 44

1. The implementation of services for women and children victims of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the PPT.
2. The PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) is an integrated part and constitutes one unit with the PPT that provides integrated services for witnesses and/or victims of the crime of trafficking in persons.
3. The implementation of services for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. providing fast and free services to victims; b. providing protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;
c. cooperating with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for victims;
d. networking with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperating with witness and victim protection institutions, social shelters or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for health recovery of victims;
e. providing convenience, comfort and safety for victims;
f. maintaining the confidentiality of victims; andg. providing fulfillment of legal assistance for victims.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures and mechanisms for implementing the PPT shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 24

1. The implementation of Protection and empowerment of children in the field of social welfare as referred to in Article 13 letter d, the Regional Government is obliged to organize social welfare for:a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of abduction and child victims of trafficking in persons;
c. child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse (NAPZA);
d. children infected with HIV/AIDS;
e. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;
f. street children;
g. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters;
h. children with disabilities;
i. children of migrant worker families;
j. children living in or around prostitution locations; and  k. child victims of other maltreatment.
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services: a health;

b education;

c legal aid;

d assistance;

e social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f social rehabilitation;

g empowerment;

h social assistance; and/or i reintegration.

Article 26

Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in Article 25 is in the form of: a counseling to stakeholders about children's rights;

b assistance with psychological, medical and legal services and socio-economic reintegration;

c empowering families through provision of training, business capital stimulus and assistance;

d scholarships to continue formal education to higher levels; and e non-formal education and skills training for those who do not pursue formal education.

Article 35

In addition to the Regional Government, prevention efforts as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. the community;
c. educational institutions; andd. Non-Governmental Organizations.

Article 34

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women and children shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government and coordinated by the technical SKPD.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by:a. providing guidance towards a harmonious household integrity;
b. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence; c. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
d. establishing a violence prevention system;
e. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; andf. providing critical education regarding the rights of women and children for the community.

Article 49

1. Every operator of internet service businesses, play stations and types of children's games based on information and communication technology is required to manage and design child-friendly places.
2. Every business operator as referred to in paragraph (1) must: a arrange rooms/booths with sufficient lighting and not closed;

b not accept children wearing uniforms and/or during school hours;

c locally block sites containing elements of pornography and pornographic acts; and d limit visiting hours and supervise the use of existing facilities.

CHAPTER VI

PROHIBITIONS

Article 47

1. Every business operation whose activities may interfere with the growth and development of children is prohibited from receiving child visitors.
2. Business operations as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a discotheques;

b nightclubs;

c bars;

d adult karaoke;

e pubs;

f massage parlors;

g steam bath/sauna parlors; and h other similar business fields.

Article 36

The implementation of handling of women and children victims of violence shall be carried out with:
a. speed;
b. safety and comfort;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easy accessibility;
f. no charges; andg. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 37

Forms of handling of women and children victims of violence include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. medical rehabilitation services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services; andf. social reintegration services.

Article 39

Assistance services as referred to in Article 37 letter b include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
c. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. protecting the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by the mass media; e. providing a sense of security to victims; andf. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 46

Women and children victims of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 45, also receive special rights, as follows:
a. the right to survival;
b. the right to grow and develop;
c. the right to receive equal protection; and d. the right to participate in community life.

